  {"question": "According to arXiv 2304.06503, what challenges did the legal and computer science experts face in classifying AI systems under the AI Act, and how did their expertise affect 'not sure' tags?", "answer": "Legal experts struggled with technical details, while computer scientists found legal references challenging. This led to more 'not sure' tags from each in their weaker area.", "evidence": [{"page": 9, "quote": "the difficulty for the computer scientists lied in deeper understanding of other legal texts, as the AI Act contains many references. The legal scientist, on the other hand, was challenged by understanding very specific AI applications that lacked a proper explanation for non-domain experts."}, {"page": 12, "quote": "The legal expertise was necessary to get to the bottom of individual legal terms (such as the legal definition of the term safety component) and the many references to other legal texts. For many entries this was the reason for the two computer scientists to tag not sure (65 and 47 times respectively). The expertise in the field of computer science was necessary to understand what is behind certain technologies. For many entries this was the reason for the legal scientist to tag not sure (59 times)."}]},

  {"question": "In arXiv 2304.06503, what ambiguity is described regarding the classification of predictive maintenance products as safety components under the AI Act?", "answer": "It is unclear if predictive maintenance products are safety components, as this depends on whether classic quality assurance mechanisms remain in place or are replaced.", "evidence": [{"page": 11, "quote": "Three cases involve predictive maintenance products (ID 73, ID 367, ID 434). It is unclear whether, if these products fail, classic quality assurance mechanisms will kick in. In this case, these products would presumably not be safety components. Even if this were currently the case, it seems likely that redundant quality assurance mechanisms will be dismantled as trust in predictive maintenance products increases. In this scenario, the products would presumably be safety components. Such considerations are not taken into account by the AI act."}]},

  {"question": "According to arXiv 2304.06503, what is the definition of 'safety component' as used in the AI Act?", "answer": "A 'safety component' is a component of a product or system that fulfills a safety function or whose failure endangers health and safety of persons or property.", "evidence": [{"page": 10, "quote": "According to Art. 3 No. 14 AI Act, a \"safety component of a product or system means a component of a product or of a system which fulfills a safety function for that product or system or the failure or malfunctioning of which endangers the health and safety of persons or property\"."}]},

  {"question": "In arXiv 2304.06503, which category in Table 1 contains the largest number of cases, and what is the exact number and percentage?", "answer": "The 'low-risk' category contains the largest number of cases: 315 cases, which is 61.28%.", "evidence": [{"page": 9, "quote": "| Number of cases that would be categorized as low-risk                            | 315 | (61,28%) |"}]},

  {"question": "In arXiv 2304.06503, which use case involved AI-supported production of plasma, and what was the main technical challenge described?", "answer": "ID 42 involved AI-supported plasma production, with the main challenge being compensating for environmental influences on plasma quality using intelligent algorithms.", "evidence": [{"page": 15, "quote": "ID 4220 : Die Forschungsgruppe Laser- und Plasmatechnologie ... Um diese und weitere Einfl¨usse bei der Plasmaerzeugung zu kompensieren, werden in der Forschungsgruppe Sensoren zur schnellen Detektion von Plasmaeigenschaften entwickelt. ... Auf Basis der Sensordaten, entwickelt die Forschungsgruppe intelligente Algorithmen, welche Plasmaquellen dauerhaft in einem Arbeitspunkt ausregeln k¨onnen."} ]},

  {"question": "According to arXiv 2304.06503, which use case focused on creating high-resolution sea ice maps for navigation, and what technology was used?", "answer": "ID 141 used neural networks to distinguish sea ice classes on SAR images and create high-resolution ice maps for navigation support.", "evidence": [{"page": 16, "quote": "ID 14124: Mittels neuronaler Netzwerke werden Signaturen von verschiedenen Meereisklassen auf SAR Bildern vollautomatisch unterschieden und so hochaufl¨osende Eiskarten in naheEchtzeit erstellt. ... Auf Basis dieser Daten werden Risikokarten erstellt, die zur Navigationsunterst¨utzung verwendet werden k¨onnen oder auf deren Basis direkt Routenempfehlungen gegeben werden."}]}
